    question_en: "A renewable-energy monitoring company operates a global web application that tracks real-time power generation data from solar and wind farms worldwide. The frontend consists of static dashboard components, while the backend relies on multiple microservices running on Amazon EC2 instances across multiple Availability Zones to process telemetry metrics (`/api/telemetry/*`) and dispatch turbine control commands (`/api/control/*`).\n\nGlobal energy operators are experiencing high latency when loading the dashboard and interacting with the APIs. Additionally, the security team has identified repeated SQL injection attempts and high-volume automated scraping attacks targeting the public API endpoints.\n\nWhich architectural solution will provide the lowest latency for global users, route traffic efficiently to the backend microservices, and protect the application against these web attacks with the least administrative effort?",
