    explanation_en: {
      "summary": "Publishing events to an Amazon SNS topic with dedicated Amazon SQS queues subscribed for each backend system implements the SNS Fan-Out pattern, decoupling publishers and consumers while allowing each consumer to buffer messages and scale independently. Applying an SNS subscription filter policy to the Local Store Pickup Dispatcher queue subscription ensures that only in-store pickup events are delivered without requiring custom application logic or additional compute overhead.",
      "options": [
        {
          "correct": false,
          "reason": "Using an Application Load Balancer with path-based routing is unsuitable because ALBs handle synchronous request forwarding rather than asynchronous event broadcasting. An ALB forwards each incoming request to a single target group, making it impossible to fan out the same inventory deduction event to multiple backend systems simultaneously. Additionally, ALBs do not provide durable message buffering or queuing to absorb traffic spikes and accommodate systems processing at different speeds."
        },
        {
          "correct": true,
          "reason": "Publishing events to an Amazon SNS topic subscribed by dedicated Amazon SQS queues for each backend system correctly implements the SNS Fan-Out pattern, enabling decoupled event broadcasting where each downstream system receives its own copy of the events. Dedicated SQS queues buffer messages during traffic surges, allowing each backend system to scale, poll, and process messages independently at its own processing rate without message loss. Furthermore, attaching an SNS subscription filter policy to the Local Store Pickup Dispatcher queue natively filters messages at the messaging layer, minimizing operational overhead and compute costs."
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "reason": "Directly subscribing AWS Lambda functions to the SNS topic to push events synchronously into downstream databases lacks durable message buffering between the event source and consumers. During intense traffic spikes, synchronous invocations and direct database writes can overwhelm backend databases, exhaust Lambda concurrency, or cause message loss if downstream systems experience latency or failure. In addition, implementing custom code logic in Lambda for message filtering introduces unnecessary compute overhead and maintenance compared to native SNS subscription filter policies."
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "reason": "Publishing events to a single shared Amazon SQS queue fails because SQS follows a point-to-point messaging pattern where each message is delivered to and processed by only one consumer. When all backend systems poll the same queue concurrently, they compete for messages, resulting in each system receiving only a disjoint subset of inventory events rather than all systems processing the full set of necessary events."
        }
      ]
    }
